.he "label (1)                  9/25/26                   label (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
label
- read and write a hard disk's label
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
label device
.br
label -w cylinders/heads/sectors device
.br
label -s device slice start [ end ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Label reads or writes the label of a hard disk: the description of the
drive that the disk carries itself.  It holds the geometry - cylinders,
heads and sectors per track - the roll, the size of the filesystem that
was put on the disk, the boot extent, and a table of eight slices.
.sp
The label is in the second half of the boot sector, the sector the rom
reads: physical cylinder 0, head 0, sector 0.  It begins at byte 256 so
that the first half can hold boot code, and only the bytes from 256 on
are ever changed, so the boot half survives every write label makes.
.sp
The device named must be the whole-disk slice, which is
.bd
c
: hd0c, hd1c, ide0c.  Every other slice is placed by the table in the
label itself, so block 0 of one is somewhere in the middle of a
filesystem - and a label written there would be written over that
filesystem.  Slice
.bd
c
is the one slice the table does not place: it starts at cylinder 0 of
the drive and is never rolled, on every disk, so its block 0 is physical
block 0 and reading it is reading the label.  That is what lets label
work on a drive it knows nothing about; a program that needed the
geometry to find the geometry would be no use at all.  Label refuses to
write to any other slice.
.sp
With no options label writes nothing.  It reads block 0, reports what
the label says, and exits non-zero if there is no label there.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-w cylinders/heads/sectors	Declare the drive geometry, written as three
decimal numbers separated by slashes.  None may be zero: the driver
refuses a label with a zero in it.
.sp
On a disk that already has a label, only these three numbers change.
The roll, the boot extent, the filesystem size and the slice table are
left exactly as they were, because they describe what is on the disk and
correcting a geometry does not put it there.
.sp
On a disk with no label, this is the write that describes it for the
first time.  A fresh label is written with the roll set to half the
cylinders, which is what the driver assumes for a disk with no label, so
the disk maps the same way after the label as it did before it and
nothing declared here can move data that is already on the drive.
.sp
There is no way yet to set the roll to anything else, so this form
cannot describe a disk whose filesystem is placed by an offset rather
than by a roll.

.ti -10
-s device slice start [ end ]	Set one slice of the table.  The slice is
named by letter a to h, or by number 0 to 7 as a minor number encodes
it; the start and the end are cylinder numbers, and the end is
inclusive.  With no end the slice runs to the last cylinder of the
drive.
.sp
The extent is stored as an offset and a length, so an end is converted:
"2 9000" is stored as offset 2, length 8999.  A slice whose entry is
left at zero and zero is the whole drive.
.sp
Slice
.bd
c
is accepted but nothing is stored for it that means anything: it is
always the whole drive from cylinder 0, so its entry is not consulted.
.sp
A slice that runs past the last cylinder is refused, and nothing is
written - not that entry, and not a geometry named on the same command
line either, since the sector is written once, at the end.  The driver
refuses to open such a slice at mount, so a label that named one would be
a disk contradicting itself.  Use -w first if the drive is really larger
than the label says it is.
.sp
-s needs a label to put the slice in.  On a disk with none, use -w
first.  Both may appear on one command line, and the sector is written
once.

.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
A drive gets one mapping, and the driver keeps it per drive rather than
per open.  A drive that is already open - the root filesystem, most
often - cannot be opened again through another slice, and label reports
that as a busy device.  Label the drive the system is not running from.
.sp
A label carries geometry and no timing.  The delay between step pulses,
the cylinder where write pre-compensation starts and the one where low
current starts come from the driver's table of drive models, chosen by
the drive's device number, and there is nowhere in a label to put them.
A geometry written here that disagrees with the row the device number
names is therefore mapped by its label and timed by that row, and the
kernel says so, once per open:
.sp
.in +5
mw1: label 925/9/17 disagrees with table 306/6/17, using label
.in -5
.sp
The label is what wins for the geometry, because the disk is the only
thing that knows how it was written.
.sp
Slices are cylinder-granular.  The table says where a slice begins and
how many cylinders it runs; the last cylinder of one slice and the first
of the next cannot share.
.sp
The label of a disk made before this tool existed is the label the
driver wrote: its table is empty, every entry meaning the whole drive
with the disk's roll.  Reading one with no options shows that, and it is
not an error - a disk made today and a disk made years ago both describe
themselves, and the description is what label prints.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
mkfs (1), icheck (1), filesystem (5)
